WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:03.570
There's a lot of hard problems in
science but only one of them is called

00:00:03.570 --> 00:00:07.589
the hard problem and that's
consciousness. How does the big pink

00:00:07.589 --> 00:00:12.630
squishy thing inside my noggin interpret
my entire experience of life? To find

00:00:12.630 --> 00:00:16.379
that out I have trapped a neuroscientist
in an elevator so I'm gonna go get her

00:00:16.380 --> 00:00:24.240
to explain. We better go she's been in there a while.

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:31.560
Hello!

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:35.360
So one of my favorite things is
that I just get to come in and ask a

00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:41.610
bunch of really difficult questions and
somebody else has to answer them and I

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:44.580
hear that what I'm asking today is
actually sometimes called the hard

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:49.019
problem. Yeah it's a really hard problem this one. People have been trying to work

00:00:49.019 --> 00:00:53.550
this problem out for thousands of years.
Yeah. Great well, you have an elevator

00:00:53.550 --> 00:01:00.239
ride to try and explain it to me. I'll do my
best. Okay ready? What is

00:01:00.239 --> 00:01:03.960
consciousness? Well from a
neurobiological perspective

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:08.800
consciousness is our kind of brains'
awareness, being aware of our inner self ,

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:12.750
our inner world and the world around us.
Okay that sounds pretty simple. What

00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:18.060
makes it hard? Well we don't exactly know
how the brain creates that experience. It

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:21.869
doesn't really seem to exist in any
specific part of the brain. Instead it's

00:01:21.869 --> 00:01:25.080
something to do with different regions
and different networks in the brain

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:29.039
having conversations and sending
messages back and forward. One

00:01:29.039 --> 00:01:33.030
really nice analogy is to think about it
as being like a theater. So we have a

00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:39.990
stage with a spotlight and... (I'll get the next one.) Sorry! ... Whatever is on the stage in the light is what's

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:44.369
in our conscious awareness. What we kind
of noticing at that point of time and

00:01:44.369 --> 00:01:47.729
then you've got what's in the wings and what's backstage and the orchestra

00:01:47.729 --> 00:01:51.569
pit and all that's kind of going on. So
we might even have thoughts happening

00:01:51.569 --> 00:01:55.830
backstage we're not aware of. So
backstage is subconsciousness? That's one

00:01:55.830 --> 00:01:59.250
way to think about it but you know when
you have an aha moment or an insight? That

00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:03.390
maybe when a thought that's been
backstage kind of comes into our

00:02:03.390 --> 00:02:07.110
conscious awareness. Okay yep. What's
unconsciousness then? Yeah that is a really

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:11.489
good question and it's really hard from
a neuroscience perspective to measure

00:02:11.489 --> 00:02:17.370
that because... Oh we're at the top. We're
halfway through. Yeah seems totally easy.

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:21.840
It's fine. We all experience being
unconscious everyday when we like in

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:26.450
deep sleep or when someone's had a
general anesthetic or if you know

00:02:26.450 --> 00:02:30.500
someone's in a coma. I think it's important to define consciousness and understand

00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:35.540
unconsciousness because that is going to
inform end-of-life decision making. Is

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:38.450
this stuff going on in their
subconscious that we just can't measure?

00:02:38.450 --> 00:02:42.410
So is it possible for us to create
consciousness through like AI or

00:02:42.410 --> 00:02:50.680
anything like that? If that fits a lot of
the definitions of consciousness... Oh my

00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:52.640
gosh!

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:57.200
Oh my god!

00:02:57.220 --> 00:03:01.670
What happened? Did...? I was very
conscious of that experience. That sensation... Mmm?

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:07.320
Hi. Hello? We're stuck in the elevator. It's not moving.

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:12.720
Please come back.

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:20.960
 

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:23.920
I'd like to see our faces when that happened.

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:32.940
I think I think it's working again. Okay
let's try. Oh yep it's moving. I think.

00:03:32.940 --> 00:03:40.840
We're good. It's not going really fast like it did. Okay - consciousness...Tell me. Where were we?

00:03:41.420 --> 00:03:43.480
So what's happening in the brain

00:03:43.480 --> 00:03:45.460
when you're conscious versus unconscious? What does that actually look like? Well

00:03:45.460 --> 00:03:49.960
we can use fMRI or brain imaging to get
a look at what neural signatures look

00:03:49.960 --> 00:03:55.210
like. In someone who's unconscious signals are coming into the brain but they're

00:03:55.210 --> 00:03:59.920
not moving around. They're decoupled. The
conversations are sort of stopped. So

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:03.550
what does that mean? So there's still
messages being transmitted but then

00:04:03.550 --> 00:04:06.400
they're not going anywhere else? The
different parts of the brain aren't in

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:10.090
communication with each other and that's
why we think it's that communication,

00:04:10.090 --> 00:04:14.500
those networks talking, that's where
consciousness is somehow emerging. Is

00:04:14.500 --> 00:04:18.790
there a difference between unconscious
in say a medically induced coma versuses

00:04:18.790 --> 00:04:24.280
sleep? That's a really good question. If
someone's in deep deep deep sleep we

00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:29.140
don't see the same neural signatures that
we do in someone who is anesthetized but

00:04:29.140 --> 00:04:32.980
someone who is anesthetized does
have a similar neural signature to

00:04:32.980 --> 00:04:36.520
someone who is perhaps in a vegetative
state or a coma. Are humans the only

00:04:36.520 --> 00:04:41.830
things that are conscious because I
would instinctively say "no" but... Probably

00:04:41.830 --> 00:04:46.660
not because we think that our
neurobiology creates consciousness but

00:04:46.660 --> 00:04:51.640
whether or not other animals like my dog
for example has the ability to reflect

00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:55.570
on his own thoughts? One of the problems
with consciousness is we rely on people

00:04:55.570 --> 00:05:00.490
being able to explain their experience of
it to us and dogs don't have language to

00:05:00.490 --> 00:05:03.580
be able to do that. And everyone
experiences stuff differently? I guess

00:05:03.580 --> 00:05:08.050
that's why it is such a hard problem
because it comes back so much to our own

00:05:08.050 --> 00:05:12.610
personal experiences and sense of who we
are. We now have the shared experience of

00:05:12.610 --> 00:05:16.810
getting stuck in this elevator for a
short time together. Yeah with that whole drop and

00:05:16.810 --> 00:05:20.900
that sudden stop and that whole life death thing. That was scary and unexpected.

00:05:21.980 --> 00:05:23.360
Thank you so much

00:05:28.220 --> 00:05:29.340
Can you open the door?

00:05:30.380 --> 00:05:34.540
When I walked out of the lift on that
one it seemed pretty simple and on

00:05:34.540 --> 00:05:38.480
further reflection I think that's
because humans all have a pretty

00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:41.930
innate idea of what consciousness is. We
all experience it? But if you were to

00:05:41.930 --> 00:05:46.639
think about someone in a vegetative
state, in a coma, that you know doesn't

00:05:46.639 --> 00:05:51.800
seem to have much brain activity you
would not necessarily feel a whole lot

00:05:51.800 --> 00:05:55.669
of moral qualms about giving them a
quiet end to life. You would often I

00:05:55.669 --> 00:06:01.460
guess think of it as being quite kind. But is that right if we can't measure it?

00:06:01.460 --> 00:06:05.090
If we can't measure if someone's
conscious we just know that they're

00:06:05.090 --> 00:06:08.810
unresponsive and they don't have a lot
of brain activity and we choose to end

00:06:08.810 --> 00:06:12.620
their life, is that the right thing to do?
Could they still be conscious and we

00:06:12.620 --> 00:06:16.160
just don't know it? So I think
consciousness is a really big tricky one.

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:19.669
A lot trickier than I thought it was
when we started and I think we're gonna

00:06:19.669 --> 00:06:22.980
need a taller building for this one.

